- `action(n14, "End"), action(n15, "End"), action(n16, "End")`: Hallucinated executable tasks; the source text defines termination as a workflow state ("the process comes to an end"), not as distinct operational steps to be performed before reaching the end nodes.
- `end(e3)` (reached via `n13`): Violates the explicit completion rule "once the payment is sent, the process comes to an end"; the model incorrectly halts execution after the second communication of problems ("Otherwise, communicate any shipment problems") instead of allowing re-evaluation or continuation until payment successfully occurs.
- `defines(v2, "Shipment okay", {...})` and `defines(v3, "Shipment okay", {...})`: Semantic conflation; identical variable names are assigned to two distinct sequential evaluations ("check if the shipment is okay" vs. "checking the shipment status again"), failing to preserve their separate decision contexts as described.
- `action(n5, "Any previous shipment problems")`: Misclassified node type/payload; the description frames this as a conditional evaluation triggering a gateway branch ("In case there were any shipment problems..."), not as a standalone process task/activity.